For the exercise, add twice to Ari, then reverse the list. Bea must still show 0 and Ari must still show 2. Repair only the key choice in the supplied list.

The supplied reversal uses [...current].reverse(). Spread creates a new array container with the same person objects. reverse() reverses that new array in place and returns it; calling current.reverse() instead would mutate the state array. The setter’s updater receives pending state and returns the copied order. This copy is shallow; the person objects are not changed here.

A small ordinary JavaScript example isolates the array operation, without another component:
```javascript
const order = ["Ari", "Bea"];
const reversed = [...order].reverse();
console.log(order.join(","));
console.log(reversed.join(","));
```
The first output is Ari,Bea and the second is Bea,Ari. join(",") returns comma-separated text from each array, without changing it; console.log prints that text. The copied array reversed, while order kept its original order.

useContext reads the closest matching provider above that component. A nested provider can override the value for its own descendants.

Compare a consumer outside a provider with one inside:
```react
const LanguageContext = React.createContext("English");
function Greeting() {
  const language = React.useContext(LanguageContext);
  return <p>{language}</p>;
}
function App() {
  return <main><Greeting /><LanguageContext.Provider value="Spanish"><Greeting /></LanguageContext.Provider></main>;
}
```
The paragraphs read English, then Spanish. The provider changes only its descendants, not the context fallback.

Each call gets its own state. Sharing a hook definition shares logic, not one shared stored value.

Change only the initial argument to true:
```react
function useToggle(initial) {
  const [enabled, setEnabled] = React.useState(initial);
  return [enabled, () => setEnabled(value => !value)];
}
function App() {
  const [enabled, toggle] = useToggle(true);
  return <button onClick={toggle}>{enabled ? "On" : "Off"}</button>;
}
```
This instance starts On; one click shows Off. useToggle(initial) returns the current Boolean and an action function. React invokes that function on the click; it queues the opposite pending value. The initial argument seeds useState on mount, not on every later render. In the separate useCounter exercise, reset explicitly uses the initial argument from the current render when that action is invoked; initialization and an explicit reset are different operations.

